id: pm-qmb6h8
title: Distinguish Vitest coverage-threshold exits from test assertion failures in combined runner verdicts
description: "A focused canonical coverage invocation reports seven test files and all 199 tests passed, but Vitest exits nonzero for the unchanged global coverage thresholds. run-tests.mjs treats that exit as a test-execution failure, combines it with the exact-source coverage refusal, and prints Test execution and exact coverage both failed with exit3. Preserve fail-closed coverage while classifying assertion execution independently from Vitest threshold exit semantics."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags: []
created_at: "2026-11-09T10:23:30.869Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-10T08:08:10.361Z"
closed_at: "2026-11-10T07:37:42.980Z"
completed_at: "2026-11-10T07:37:42.980Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 60
acceptance_criteria: Reproduce all-pass focused tests plus coverage-only failure; retain nonzero exact coverage admission and unchanged full denominator; distinguish test-only coverage-only and combined failures without parsing fragile human log strings
parent: pm-u9d0
risk: medium
confidence: 95
resolution: "Added an invocation-owned minimal Vitest reporter, defensive receipt classification, explicit reporter retention, current public reporter types and native file-level MCP attribution; documented exit semantics, retained failed attempts, sanitized local path evidence, and registered typed terminal-defect gate evidence."
expected_result: "Distinguish assertion outcomes from process and exact-coverage refusal, preserve strict admission and complete context, and pass terminal-defect governance after closure."
actual_result: "Complete monolithic verification passes 12383 assertions with exact 100/100/100/100 coverage across 843 source files. Real-process negative controls retain deliberate exit-3 coverage refusal. Static quality passed before closure; the later CI ratchet found only two missing formal fields, now supplied without changing code or policy. Final post-close gate verification follows."
